::ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which sequence is arithmetic? {
= 4, 9, 14, 19#Correct. Each term increases by the constant difference 5.
~ 4, 8, 16, 32#This is geometric because each term is multiplied by 2.
~ 4, 7, 12, 19#The differences are not constant.
~ 4, 16, 64, 256#This is geometric because each term is multiplied by 4.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which sequence is geometric? {
~ 3, 6, 9, 12#This has a constant difference, so it is arithmetic.
= 3, 6, 12, 24#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 2.
~ 3, 7, 11, 15#This has a constant difference of 4.
~ 3, 5, 8, 12#The differences are not constant and there is no constant ratio.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For 7, 11, 15, 19, ..., what is the common difference? {
~ 3#The terms do not increase by 3 each time.
~ 7#The first term is 7, but the common difference compares consecutive terms.
= 4#Correct. Each term increases by 4.
~ 11#This is the second term, not the common difference.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For 2, 10, 50, 250, ..., what is the common ratio? {
~ 2#The first term is 2, but the ratio compares consecutive terms.
~ 8#This is the first difference, not a constant ratio.
~ 10#The second term is not the ratio.
= 5#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 5.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the 6th term of 5, 8, 11, 14, ...? {
= 20#Correct. This arithmetic sequence has d=3, so a<sub>6</sub>=5+5(3)=20.
~ 17#This is the 5th term, not the 6th.
~ 23#This adds one extra common difference.
~ 30#This multiplies instead of adding the common difference.
}
